    "available_at": "2026-11-20T09:47:27.160120+00:00",
    "body": "The Royal Australian Navy replenishment ship HMAS Stalwart's embarked MH-60R helicopter shown during a regional presence deployment. (Photo: Australian Department of Defence)\nMELBOURNE – Australia has received the first two of 13 additional Sikorsky MH-60R Seahawk naval helicopters, expanding the Royal Australian Navy’s maritime aviation fleet.\nThe helicopters were delivered to the Royal Australian Navy (RAN) base HMAS Albatross near Nowra in New South Wales, Au stralia’s Department of Defence announced Thursday.\nThe delivery marks the first pair of 13 helicopters purchased from Lockheed Martin subsidiary Sikorsky Australia in 2022 under Canberra’s Project SEA9100 Phase 1, which aims to expand the RAN’s helicopter fleet. The acquisition included 12 additional MH-60Rs and a 13th aircraft to replace one lost during operations in 2021.\nThe helicopters will increase the RAN’s Seahawk fleet fleet from 23 to 36 aircraft, with the remaining 11 helicopters are expected to arrive by early 2028, according to Sikorsky Australia.\nThe MH-60R is primarily designed for anti-submarine and anti-surface warfare, but can also conduct intelligence, surveillance and reconnaissance; maritime strike; search and rescue; and logistics support missions. The helicopters are equipped with a sensor suite, as well as Mark 54 lightweight torpedoes and Hellfire air-to-surface missiles.\nIncreasing the Royal Australian Navy’s Seahawk fleet “will allow the ADF to effectively operate in complex maritime environments and enhance its ability to defend Australia’s maritime approaches and contribute to regional security across the Indo-Pacific,” the Australian Defence Department said in a statement.\nCliff Kyle, sustainment director at Lockheed Martin Australia, said the RAN’s expanded Seahawk fleet will extend the helicopter’s “proven capability across more Navy assets and operational environments, further strengthening Australia’s ability to detect and respond to surface and subsurface threats.”\nThe first two helicopters completed flight testing in the United States before being transported to Australia, according to Lockheed Martin. The aircraft will undergo RAN-specific modifications before entering service, which is expected in April 2027, Deputy Prime Minister and Defense Minister Richard Marles said in a statement.\nAustralia initially sought 12 additional MH-60Rs for its fleet, with the $503 million Foreign Military Sales contract for the helicopters awarded by Naval Air Systems Command in August 2022.\nHowever, the 13th helicopter was subsequently added to the contract to replace an MH-60R lost while attempting to land on board the destroyer HMAS Brisbane in the Philippine Sea in October 2021.\nBreaking Defense Video\nCandidates are lining up to be the next top HASC Republican.\nWatch Now »\nThe real-world missile defense lessons from Ukraine and Iran — and how they matter for China\nWatch Now »\nA $1.1 trillion Pentagon budget plan sets up a fight on Capitol Hill\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon fails another audit. Can it turn things around by 2028?\nWatch Now »\nA new Air Force stunt plane, plus a shakeup for special operations\nWatch Now »\nNATO nations have a missile crisis. How did it happen, and can it be fixed?\nWatch Now »\nA Pentagon impeachment showdown, plus defense funding in limbo\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nThe push to give dismounted forces direct access to LEO and MEO networks [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon wants a massive new military base. Where will it go?\nWatch Now »\nWhat now? Budget fight, Hegseth impeachment on deck for defense-focused lawmakers after midterms\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
